<section>
    <h3>HAR: HTTP Archive</h3>
    <p class="subtitle" style="font-size: 0.55em;">The W3C JSON format for archiving HTTP exchanges: readable, reviewed in a pull request like code, opened by browser devtools</p>
    <pre><code class="language-json" data-trim data-line-numbers="1-18|3-9|6-8|10-17|13-16">
{
  "startedDateTime": "2026-09-03T14:07:14.638Z",
  "request": {
    "method": "POST",
    "url": "https://auth.example.com/oauth/token",
    "postData": {
      "text": "grant_type=client_credentials&amp;client_secret=%5BREDACTED%5D"
    }
  },
  "response": {
    "status": 200,
    "headers": [{"name": "content-type", "value": "application/json"}],
    "content": {
      "mimeType": "application/json",
      "text": "{\"access_token\":\"[REDACTED]\",\"expires_in\":3600}"
    }
  }
}
    </code></pre>
    <p class="subtitle" style="font-size: 0.5em;">Simplified excerpt of a file written by the recorder. A binary body is stored as base64.</p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>HAR, for HTTP Archive. It's a JSON format for archiving HTTP exchanges. It's readable, it's reviewed in a pull request like code, and you've probably met it already without knowing: in the Network tab of your browser, the "Save all as HAR" button is exactly this format. So a file recorded by your tests can be opened in your devtools.</p>
        <p>Let's look at an excerpt.</p>
        <p>[click] We have the request: the method, POST, and the URL, here an OAuth endpoint.</p>
        <p>[click] And the request body, in postData. Look closely at the client_secret: it's [REDACTED]. Keep that in mind, we'll come back to it, it's one of the key points of this talk.</p>
        <p>[click] Then the response: the status, 200…</p>
        <p>[click] … and its content. Here again, the access_token is redacted, but expires_in is intact.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
